{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 26, :comments ["<b>But you shall keep.</b> The reason the verse says the word <b>you</b> and was not satisfied with addressing them directly with ״וּשְׁמַרְתֶּם״ — it appears that this commandment comes to warn concerning the statutes and the judgments, that they not be nullified by any of those who are commanded in them. The intention is that a person should not say that he need only be careful that the commandments not be nullified through him, but if they are nullified by others he need not be concerned; therefore it says <b>et My statutes</b> etc., meaning, that they not be nullified by any of those commanded in them. And since this commandment falls only upon the great ones, therefore it says ״you״ — speaking to Moses and his court, for the courts of Israel must keep a watchful eye lest a single commandment of the Torah be nullified among the children of the covenant. And in Torat Kohanim they said, of blessed memory: ״וּשְׁמַרְתֶּם אַתֶּם״ — you are the ones fit to guard them, for you were the first to open them [to observance], and thus it says (Song of Songs 4:12): ״גַּן נָעוּל אֲחוֹתִי כַלָּה גַּל נָעוּל מַעְיָן חָתוּם״, end quote. The meaning is: here HaShem brought the warning against forbidden relations close to habitual nature, and said to them that this commandment of guarding against forbidden relations is not a new and foreign thing among Israel, for they on their own even before they were commanded were separated from forbidden relations, as it is written: ״גַּן נָעוּל אֲחוֹתִי כַלָּה״, and this is what He alluded to by saying <b>you</b>, meaning, what you already kept on your own." "<b>The native and the stranger.</b> Our Rabbis of blessed memory interpreted (Torat Kohanim): ״אֶזְרָח״ — this is a native; ״הָאֶזְרָח״ — to include the wives of natives; ״גֵּר״ — this is the stranger; ״הַגֵּר״ — to include the wives of strangers, etc., end quote. The reason the verse needed to spell out all these particulars is lest one err from the word ״you״ and say that it comes to exclude some particular from among those listed — a hermeneutical derivation, etc." "<b>Further,</b> it appears that the verse intends to inform us that if Israel and the strangers keep all the statutes and the judgments, there is no power in the deeds of the nations, even though they abominate their deeds, to cause the land to vomit out its inhabitants. And it was specifically before Israel entered the land that the land vomited out the nations on account of the abominable deed, but after Israel's entry into the land the matter depends only on Israel and the stranger; therefore it specified them, that their deeds would diminish [the impact of] the nations' [deeds]." "<b>And you will find</b> that the verse says (Exodus 23:33) ״לֹא יֵשְׁבוּ בְּאַרְצְךָ פֶּן יַחֲטִיאוּ אוֹתְךָ לִי״, and Rambam and the Sefer Mitzvot Gadol wrote that a gentile who did not accept upon himself the seven commandments is forbidden to dwell in the land of Israel on account of ״פֶּן יַחֲטִיאוּ״ etc. From this you learn that were it not for the reason of ״פֶּן יַחֲטִיאוּ״, even if he does not accept upon himself the seven commandments and abominates his deeds, there is no concern that the land would vomit them out on their account."], :book :leviticus, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 18, :notes {0 "The midrashic play on אַתֶּם ('you') as related to פְּתַחְתֶּם ('you opened') and נָאִים ('fit/fitting') depends on Rabbinic Hebrew wordplay that does not carry fully into English; the literal rendering preserves the original terms."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:58:46.628554711Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}